{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2157-11-07 01:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee. I understand you came in with abdominal pain and nausea. Can you tell me more about what’s been going on?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2157-11-07 01:18:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, it’s my usual pain—epigastric and right upper quadrant, radiating to my back. It’s been worse the last two days. I haven’t eaten anything in a few days because I feel so nauseous."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2157-11-07 01:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. And you had that ERCP a while back where they placed a stent in your pancreatic duct. Are you here just for the stent removal, or has the pain been different this time?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2157-11-07 01:21:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "It’s my usual pain, but I’ve also had a low-grade fever and more diarrhea—about five loose stools a day. No blood though."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2157-11-07 01:22:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay, thanks. In the ER, your blood sugar was very high—698—and your labs showed some acid buildup, so you were in diabetic ketoacidosis. We gave you fluids and insulin, and your levels have improved."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2157-11-07 01:23:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, that makes sense. My appetite’s been off, so I probably didn’t take enough insulin. Is that dangerous?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2157-11-07 01:25:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It can be, but we caught it early. Your blood sugar is down to 335 now, and your acid levels are improving. You’re no longer in DKA, which is good."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2157-11-07 01:26:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, good. What about the stent? I was supposed to come back to get it removed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2157-11-07 01:27:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’ve scheduled an EGD for later today to remove the pancreatic stent. It’s a quick procedure. We’ll use an endoscope to reach the stent and take it out."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2157-11-07 01:28:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will it hurt? I’m really sensitive to pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2157-11-07 01:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll be sedated, so you won’t feel anything. We’ll also give you pain medication afterward if needed. Your pain has been managed with hydromorphone and ibuprofen, and we’ll continue that as needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2157-11-07 12:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi again. The EGD went well. We successfully removed the pancreatic stent. It was still in place at the major papilla, and there were no complications."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2157-11-07 12:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. Does that mean I’ll feel better soon?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2157-11-07 12:03:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It should help prevent future pancreatitis episodes. Your pain is likely from chronic pancreatitis, and the stent was only temporary. The ERCP from before showed no stones or blockages—just an irregular, mildly dilated duct, which fits with your diagnosis."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2157-11-07 12:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So nothing’s changed? I’ll still have pain?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2157-11-07 12:06:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Unfortunately, yes. This is chronic pain, and we focus on managing it. You’re already on gabapentin and mirtazapine, which help with nerve-related pain. We’ll keep using your usual medications, including the hydromorphone as needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2157-11-07 18:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve tolerated dinner well, which is great. Your blood sugar is more stable, and you’re not showing signs of infection. We’re planning to discharge you soon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2157-11-07 18:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. What should I do at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2157-11-07 18:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Keep taking your medications as prescribed. For insulin, start with 20 units of Lantus at bedtime. If your appetite improves, you may need to increase it—possibly up to 40 units. Use your Humalog as directed by your endocrinologist."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2157-11-07 18:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And if the pain flares up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2157-11-07 18:07:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You can take hydromorphone every three hours as needed, or ibuprofen every six hours. If nausea returns, use the prochlorperazine. And call your doctor if things worsen or you can’t keep fluids down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2157-11-07 18:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. Should I follow up with anyone?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2157-11-07 18:10:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, stay in touch with your primary care provider and endocrinology team. They can help adjust your insulin. And keep managing your asthma with Advair and albuterol as needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2157-11-07 19:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just a final check—your mental status is clear, you’re alert, and you’re walking fine. We’re clearing you to go home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2157-11-07 19:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I’m glad the stent’s out and I’m stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2157-11-07 19:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Take care of yourself, monitor your sugars, and reach out if you need help. You’re doing well."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2157-11-07 19:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "One last thing—should I worry about the diarrhea?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2157-11-07 19:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s likely related to your gastroparesis or recent fasting. Keep hydrated and eat small, bland meals. If it persists, let your doctor know, but it’s not a major concern now."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2157-11-07 01:17:00",
    "admission_end": "2157-11-07 20:35:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient was admitted with abdominal pain, nausea, and uncontrolled type 1 diabetes that presented with diabetic ketoacidosis. She had a history of chronic pancreatitis and a temporary pancreatic stent placed after a prior ERCP. During this admission, she underwent successful removal of the pancreatic stent via EGD. Her DKA resolved with IV fluids and insulin, and she transitioned to subcutaneous insulin with instructions to titrate based on appetite and glucose monitoring. She tolerated oral intake, her pain remained consistent with her baseline chronic pattern, and she was discharged home in stable condition with ongoing management of her chronic conditions.",
    "problems": [
      "Abdominal pain, unspecified site",
      "Diabetes with ketoacidosis, type I [juvenile type], uncontrolled",
      "Chronic pancreatitis",
      "Long-term (current) use of insulin",
      "Other chronic pain",
      "Asthma, unspecified type, unspecified",
      "Diabetes with neurological manifestations, type I [juvenile type], uncontrolled",
      "Gastroparesis",
      "Borderline personality disorder"
    ]
  }
}
